## Payroll approval workflow (005)

`payroll_runs.status` moves through:

```
Submitted --approve--> Approved --release--> Released
Submitted --return---> Returned  --resubmit-> Submitted
Returned  --approve--> Approved
```

* `Submitted` — the Payroll Officer (maker) processed the period.
* `Approved` / `Released` — locked: payslips, allowances, deductions, loan
  payments and cash disbursements are refused with HTTP 409 and a formal
  adjustment is required.
* `Returned` — the Approver sent it back with remarks; the run is editable
  again and the maker can resubmit it.

`payroll_db_schema.sql` (structure only) and `payroll_db_recovery.sql`
(structure + data, including the seeded `payroll` / `approver` / `admin`
accounts) are regenerated from the live database after every migration.

The three staff logins can be (re)created idempotently with:

```
C:\xampp\php\php.exe tests\seed_staff_accounts.php
```

| Account | Role | Password |
|---------|------|----------|
| payroll | Payroll Officer (maker) | test123 (never overwritten if it already exists) |
| approver | Approver (checker) | test123 (never overwritten if it already exists) |
| admin | Administrator (technical only) | unchanged |

## Employee bank details + payment exports (006)

`employees` gained three nullable columns:

| Column | Width | Purpose |
|--------|-------|---------|
| `bank_name` | VARCHAR(100) | the receiving bank |
| `bank_account_name` | VARCHAR(120) | account holder exactly as the bank knows it |
| `bank_account_number` | VARCHAR(40) | the account the bank file credits |

* They are edited on the Employees screen (Payment Details) and are
  MAKER-writable only: `employees.php` already requires `MAKER_ROLES` for
  POST/PUT, so the Approver can read them (needed to review the bank file) but
  cannot change them. A blank field is stored as NULL.
* They are never returned by the employee portal: every `me_*` endpoint selects
  its columns explicitly, so adding the columns cannot leak them.

`api/bank_file.php` is the single export endpoint:

```
GET api/bank_file.php?run_id=<id>&format=bank|register|remittance
```

| Format | File name | Contents |
|--------|-----------|----------|
| `bank` | `bank-file_<start>_<end>.csv` | one credit line per payslip + a TOTAL row |
| `register` | `payroll-register_<start>_<end>.csv` | every payslip: basic, overtime, gross, SSS/PhilHealth/Pag-IBIG employee shares, withholding tax, other deductions, totals + a TOTAL row |
| `remittance` | `govt-remittance_<start>_<end>.csv` | SSS / PhilHealth / Pag-IBIG employee AND employer amounts per employee + a GRAND TOTAL row |

Guard and refusals:

* `Payroll Officer` and `Approver` only (200). `Employee` and
  `Administrator` -> 403.
* Run missing -> 404; `format` not one of the three -> 422.
* Run status `Returned` -> 409 for every format: its figures are not final
  until the maker resubmits.
* `format=bank` -> 422 and no file at all when ANY included employee is
  missing `bank_name` or `bank_account_number`; the message names every
  affected employee (employee number + name).
* The CSV is `text/csv` with LF line endings, fields containing a comma, quote
  or newline are quoted, and a cell starting with `=`, `+`, `-` or `@` is
  prefixed with a single quote so it can never run as a spreadsheet formula.
* Exports are GETs and the central `respond()` hook only audits non-GET
  requests, so `bank_file.php` writes its own `audit_log` row for every attempt
  (successes and refusals alike). Account numbers are never stored in it.

`tests/bank_file_test.php` covers the three formats, the missing-bank-details
refusal, the Returned guard, the role guards, the CSV escaping and the audit
rows, and restores every fixture it creates.

## Attendance corrections (007)

Before this step an employee had no recourse: `api/attendance.php` is
MAKER-writable only, the kiosk endpoint never edits a stored time, so a
forgotten scan-out was simply lost. `attendance_corrections` is the request
queue that closes that gap:

| Column | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `employee_id` | who is asking (always the portal session's employee) |
| `attendance_id` | the attendance row being fixed, NULL while none exists |
| `work_date` | the day the correction is about |
| `correction_type` | `clock_in`, `clock_out` or `both` |
| `requested_time_in` / `requested_time_out` | the times being asked for |
| `reason` | the employee's explanation (10-500 characters) |
| `status` | `Pending` -> `Approved` or `Rejected` |
| `requested_at`, `reviewed_by`, `reviewed_at`, `review_remarks` | who decided what, when, and why |

Indexes: `(status)`, `(employee_id, work_date)`, `(attendance_id)`. The
`attendance_id` foreign key is `ON DELETE SET NULL`: deleting an attendance row
is never blocked by an old request, and the request keeps its own work date and
requested times.

### Portal: `employee_portal/api/me_attendance_corrections.php`

```
GET  -> { rows: [ own requests + the attendance row's current times ], total }
POST -> { correction_id, status: "Pending", work_date, correction_type, ... }
```

* Session-scoped: the session employee is always the subject; an
  `employee_id` in the body is ignored (never read).
* `work_date` must be today or earlier and no older than 90 days -> 422.
* `reason` is required, at least 10 characters and at most 500 -> 422.
* `correction_type` must be one of the three; the time(s) that type needs are
  required, and the resolved time out must be after the time in -> 422.
* A second `Pending` request for the same employee + work date -> 409.
* A work date inside an `Approved` / `Released` payroll run -> 409, and the
  message says a formal payroll adjustment is required instead.
* A `clock_out` correction with no recorded clock-in -> 422 (file `both`).
* The portal has no central audit hook (only the admin's `respond()` has one),
  so this endpoint writes its own `audit_log` row for every filing, exactly
  like `me_profile.php`.

The portal UI is a "Request a correction" section on `attendance.html`: a
form (date, type, time in/out, reason) and a table of the employee's own
requests with status and the reviewer's remarks.

### Admin: `api/attendance_corrections.php`

```
GET   ?status= &employee_id= &from= &to= &limit= &offset=
      -> { rows, total, limit, offset }                  PAYROLL_ROLES
PATCH { correction_id, action: "approve" | "reject", remarks? }   MAKER_ROLES
```

* `GET` returns employee name/number, the current attendance times, the
  requested times, the reason, the status, the requester (`requested_at`) and
  the reviewer (`reviewed_by_name`, `reviewed_at`, `review_remarks`).
  Pending rows sort first, then the newest work date. `limit` defaults to 100
  and is clamped to 500, like `api/audit_log.php`.
* `PATCH` is the maker's: attendance is the Payroll Officer's domain, so the
  Approver can read the queue but never decide it (403).
* Rejecting requires `remarks` -> 422 without them.
* Anything that is not `Pending` -> 409, so approving twice can never apply a
  second edit.

### What "approve" does

One transaction:

1. the request is re-read `FOR UPDATE`; not `Pending` -> 409;
2. the work date is checked with `assertAttendanceDateUnlocked()`
   (`api/_payroll_helpers.php`) -> 409 on a locked date;
3. the attendance row for that employee + date is created when it does not
   exist (`scan_device_id = 'Correction'`), otherwise `time_in` / `time_out`
   are set per `correction_type`;
4. `hours_worked`, `minutes_late` and `status` are recomputed with the SAME
   arithmetic `api/scan_attendance.php` uses (below);
5. the request becomes `Approved` with `reviewed_by`, `reviewed_at` and the
   new `attendance_id`.

### The payroll lock for a date

The existing lock helpers are addressed by `run_id` / `payslip_id`; a
correction is addressed by employee + date, so `api/_payroll_helpers.php` gained
`attendanceDateLock()`, `attendanceDateLockedMessage()` and
`assertAttendanceDateUnlocked()`. A date is locked when ANY run whose
`payroll_periods` window covers it has status `Approved` or `Released`
(`PAYROLL_LOCKED_RUN_STATUSES`). `Submitted` and `Returned` runs stay editable
and a date with no run at all is always editable. The portal refuses to file
and the admin refuses to approve; both answer 409 and say a formal adjustment
is required. The portal cannot include the admin's helper file, so it has a
small copy of the same query in `me_attendance_corrections.php`
(`attendance_date_lock()`) - if the rule changes, both must change.

### Hour / late recomputation rule (shared with the kiosk)

`api/scan_attendance.php` clock-in:

```php
$workStart   = new DateTime("08:00:00");
$actualTime  = new DateTime($nowTime);
$minutesLate = 0;
if ($actualTime > $workStart) {
    $diff = $workStart->diff($actualTime);
    $minutesLate = ($diff->h * 60) + $diff->i;
}
```

`api/scan_attendance.php` clock-out:

```php
$diff         = $timeIn->diff($timeOut);
$totalMinutes = ($diff->h * 60) + $diff->i;
$netMinutes   = $totalMinutes > 300 ? $totalMinutes - 60 : $totalMinutes;
$hoursWorked  = round($netMinutes / 60, 2);
```

So `minutes_late` is the whole number of minutes past 08:00 (0 at or before
08:00), and `hours_worked` loses one 60-minute lunch only when the in/out span
is LONGER than 300 minutes (exactly 300 keeps 5.00 hours). Seconds are ignored
in both, as they are at the kiosk. `attendance_corrections.php` implements this
as `scanMinutesLate()` / `scanHoursWorked()` and the status follows the kiosk:
`Late` when late, else `Present` for a completed day or `Clocked In` for a
time-in only.

### UI

`attendance_corrections.html` + `assets/js/attendance_corrections.js` is the
approval queue: pending count, status / employee / work-date filters, before
and after times, the reason, and Approve / Reject (reject asks for remarks).
The action buttons are only rendered for the maker, and the API refuses
everyone else anyway.

`assets/js/nav.js` is the shared role-aware navigation: one `data-page` keyed
table hides the links the current role cannot open (and shows a clear
"You do not have access to this page" card when a page is opened by URL
directly). `tests/nav_matrix_test.php` proves the table covers every
`data-page` value in the HTML and matches the API guard page by page.

### Tests

`tests/attendance_correction_test.php` covers the whole round trip: filing,
the validation refusals (future date, short reason, 90-day window), the
duplicate 409, the locked-date 409, the ignored client `employee_id`, the
portal's own-only listing, approve applying (create and update) with an
independent recomputation of the hours / late minutes including the 300-minute
boundary, reject requiring remarks and leaving attendance unchanged, the
double-approve 409, the role guards, the queue filters and pagination, and one
`audit_log` row per mutation. It restores every attendance row it touches and
prints a PASS/FAIL summary.
